Package @getty/agent-bun-runtime at the repository root: package.json (Artistic-2.0, publishConfig.access public, ESM, exports only what is implemented, files allowlist, repository Getty/agent-bun-runtime), bun.lock. No runtime dependency without a reason in the report; dev dependencies named with their reason. Bundle-friendly (ADR 0003): ESM, no dynamic require, no reads of its own package files, no side effects on import. ADR 0006 says "ESM plus declarations": follow it or name the deviation.

Core (src/, host-neutral):
- main(options): Promise<number>, single response owner (handoff 6.4 cut to the MVP): read stdin, decode through the codec it is given, build the context, run the handler, validate the effect, encode, write once, return the exit code. main never calls process.exit itself; document the entry pattern.
- stdin: read one complete JSON object, not to EOF (plan, "Host checks", stdin). Bounded size. Must not depend on the host closing stdin: an entry whose stdin stays open ends once the object is read and main has returned.
- Context from explicit inputs (handoff 6.1 envelope cut to the MVP; no hidden globals; missing host data stays missing).
- Typed errors (handoff 7.5): code, message, stage; only codes the MVP uses.
- Effects no-decision and add-context (handoff 6.2: text, audience model, placement). Unsupported effect or placement is an explicit error, never a silent conversion.
- Process result: what main hands to the process (exit code plus stdout bytes, written once). Not the subprocess helper of handoff 7.3 (that is outside the MVP; own card).
- Output (k11): all hook stdout goes through one function that writes nothing for empty output (Bun.write of an empty buffer to a pipe fails with EINVAL on Windows, CI 45572032454).
- Exit codes: 0 when the effect was encoded and written; 1 for every SDK failure, typed or not, with one stderr line naming the error code and no stdout; the SDK never returns 2 for its own failures (some hosts read 2 as a block, and no MVP effect blocks). Codes stay within 0-255. Document in README.
- Codec interface the Codex codec (separate card) implements: decode native input into the envelope, encode an effect for the decoded event.

